added_lemmas.proof_tex:
Choose a law \(P\) from the nonempty class.  By \(\mathrm{def:predictable\mbox{-}class}\), this law satisfies \(\mathrm{ass:bounded\mbox{-}outcomes}\).  Thus \(-B\le Y_{ij}(a)\le B\) almost surely.  Monotonicity of conditional expectation and preservation of constants give, for every \(i\in I\), \(j\in J\), and \(a\in\{0,1\}\),
\[
 -B
 =E_P[-B\mid\mathbb G_{o(i,j)-1}]
 \le \mu^a_{ij}
 \le E_P[B\mid\mathbb G_{o(i,j)-1}]
 =B
 \qquad P\text{-almost surely}.
\]
Using the definition \(M=\mu^1-\mu^0\) and the triangle inequality therefore yields
\[
 |M_{ij}|\le2B
 \qquad P\text{-almost surely}.
\]
The same class definition also imposes \(\mathrm{ass:signal\mbox{-}floor}\).  Since every singular value is bounded by the Frobenius norm, the preceding entrywise bound gives
\[
 \lambda_{nm}
 \le\sigma_r(M)
 \le\lVert M\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le\lVert M\rVert_{\mathrm F}
 =\left(\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^m M_{ij}^2\right)^{1/2}
 \le2B\sqrt{nm}
 \qquad P\text{-almost surely}.
\]
The two endpoints of this chain are deterministic, so nonemptiness proves
\(\lambda_{nm}\le2B\sqrt{nm}\).

We next work along the stated fixed-complexity aspect-ratio path.  Positivity of \(B\) and \(\lambda_{nm}\) permits division in the signal bound and gives
\[
 \frac{B\sqrt{nm}}{\lambda_{nm}}\ge\frac12.
\]
Also
\[
 \sqrt{n^{-1}+nm^{-2}}\ge n^{-1/2}.
\]
Thus \(\mathrm{def:geometric\mbox{-}rate}\) and \(\mathrm{def:frontier\mbox{-}rate}\) imply
\[
 b_{nm}
 \ge 1\wedge
 \left\{
 \frac{K r^{3/2}\mu}{2\sqrt q}
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(en)}n}
 \right\}.
\]
The expression inside braces tends to zero because \(r,\mu,K,q\) are fixed.  It is therefore at most one eventually, proving the asserted lower bound for \(b_{nm}\).

For the other two radii, the upper aspect bound and the signal bound imply
\[
 \frac B{\lambda_{nm}}
 \ge\frac1{2\sqrt{nm}}
 \ge\frac1{2\sqrt{c_+}\,n}.
\]
Furthermore, \(n\vee m\ge n\) and
\(\log(e(n+m))\ge\log(en)\).  Retaining only the first nonnegative summand in the definition of \(a^{\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\) therefore gives
\[
 \frac B{\lambda_{nm}}
 \left\{
 \sqrt{\frac{(n\vee m)\log(e(n+m))}{q}}
 +\frac{\log(e(n+m))}{q}
 \right\}
 \ge
 \frac1{2\sqrt{c_+q}}
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(en)}n}.
\]
The right-hand side tends to zero.  Hence it is at most one eventually, and monotonicity of \(x\mapsto1\wedge x\) yields
\[
 a^{\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}
 \ge\sqrt{\frac n{\mu r}}\,
 \frac1{2\sqrt{c_+q}}
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(en)}n}
 =\frac1{2\sqrt{c_+q\mu r}}\sqrt{\log(en)}.
\]
The identical argument, retaining the \(q^{-1}\) first summand in \(d_{nm}\), gives
\[
 \frac B{\lambda_{nm}}
 \left\{
 q^{-1}\sqrt{(n\vee m)\log(e(n+m))}
 +q^{-3}\log(en)
 \right\}
 \ge
 \frac1{2q\sqrt{c_+}}
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(en)}n},
\]
and thus, eventually,
\[
 d_{nm}
 \ge\frac1{2q\sqrt{c_+\mu r}}\sqrt{\log(en)}.
\]
Since \(\log(en)\to\infty\), the two claimed nonshrinking conclusions follow.
